prompt:
Use Three.js to implement a procedural lava shader effect based on noise-driven vertex displacement and fragment shading, combined with UnrealBloomPass glow post-processing.

[Three.js Import Method (Must Be Followed Strictly)]
Use the following importmap in <head>, and import through ES Modules in <script type="module">:
<script type="importmap">
{ "imports": {
    "three": "https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/three@0.170.0/build/three.module.js",
    "three/addons/": "https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/three@0.170.0/examples/jsm/"
} }
</script>
<script type="module">
import * as THREE from 'three';
import { OrbitControls } from 'three/addons/controls/OrbitControls.js';
import { EffectComposer } from 'three/addons/postprocessing/EffectComposer.js';
import { RenderPass } from 'three/addons/postprocessing/RenderPass.js';
import { UnrealBloomPass } from 'three/addons/postprocessing/UnrealBloomPass.js';
// Write your code here
</script>

[Lava Object]
(1) Main geometry: TorusKnotGeometry(1, 0.4, 256, 64), providing a complex surface on which to display the shader effect;
(2) Use a custom ShaderMaterial containing vertexShader and fragmentShader;

[Vertex Shader - Noise Displacement]
(3) Implement a 3D Simplex Noise or classic Perlin Noise function (inline the snoise(vec3) function in GLSL);
(4) Vertex displacement: offset along the normal = displacement * snoise(position * noiseScale + time * 0.5);
(5) The displacement uniform controls the displacement amplitude;
(6) Recalculate the normal after displacement (estimate tangents by taking finite differences between the displacements of neighboring points, then obtain the normal using a cross product);

[Fragment Shader - Lava Shading]
(7) Two-layer noise blending:
  - layer1 = snoise(worldPos * noiseScale * 2.0 + time * speed * 0.3)
  - layer2 = snoise(worldPos * noiseScale * 4.0 - time * speed * 0.2)
  - combined = (layer1 + layer2 * 0.5) * 0.667, mapped to [0, 1]
(8) Color ramp:
  - t < 0.25: dark red (0.1, 0.0, 0.0) → red (0.8, 0.0, 0.0)
  - t < 0.5: red → orange (1.0, 0.5, 0.0)
  - t < 0.75: orange → yellow (1.0, 1.0, 0.0)
  - t >= 0.75: yellow → white (1.0, 1.0, 1.0)
(9) Multiply the final color by the colorIntensity uniform to control brightness;
(10) Bright regions automatically produce glow (in conjunction with UnrealBloomPass);

[Uniforms]
(11) time: float (automatically incremented to drive the animation);
(12) noiseScale: float, controlled by a slider with id="noiseScale", range 1-10, step 0.1, default 3.0;
(13) speed: float, controlled by a slider with id="speed", range 0.1-3.0, step 0.1, default 1.0;
(14) colorIntensity: float, controlled by a slider with id="intensity", range 0.5-2.0, step 0.1, default 1.0;
(15) displacement: float, controlled by a slider with id="displacement", range 0-2.0, step 0.1, default 0.5;

[Post-Processing - UnrealBloomPass]
(16) EffectComposer pipeline: RenderPass → UnrealBloomPass;
(17) Bloom strength slider with id="bloomStrength", range 0-3, step 0.1, default 1.5;
(18) Bloom threshold slider with id="bloomThreshold", range 0-1, step 0.05, default 0.3;
(19) Use the default Bloom radius value or 0.5;

[Scene Configuration]
(20) Dark background (#000000), with optional starfield particles (several hundred small white points scattered over a distant spherical surface);
(21) Use OrbitControls for rotating the view;
(22) Object self-rotation: slowly rotate around the Y-axis by 0.005 radians per frame;

[State Exposure (Required)]
(23) Update window.__3D_STATE__ every frame with the following fields:
  - noiseScale: number
  - speed: number
  - colorIntensity: number
  - displacement: number
  - bloomStrength: number
  - bloomThreshold: number
  - fps: number (average frame rate over the most recent 1 second)
  - time: number (current value of the shader's time uniform)

Output a single HTML file that can run by being opened directly in a browser.
